WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.120
Renegade!

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:06.840
2am is no time to be alone in the subway, and you're about to find out why.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:30.160
Nostalgia And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.420
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.020
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.360
And guys, today we're going to be talking about the 1970s film, The Warriors.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.500
Why did you know I was going to say that, Sean?

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.960
Because it's just The Warriors.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.820
And it has that vibe though, right?

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.560
Yeah, you're in the subway, you're beating dudes up, you're beating women up at some point.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.580
Whoa, whoa. Slow down, slow down.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.200
I'm sure there's a reasonable explanation.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.560
I hope so.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.040
I was very confused.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.800
Yeah, the box art itself for Renegade is definitely giving me the Warriors vibes here.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:11.840
I don't know if you guys saw it, but it's the red spray paint for Renegade, and they've Americanized the whole thing from the Japanese version.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.940
The Japanese version is completely different.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.140
It's worth mentioning. So we're going to be talking about the American version of the game.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.820
Hopefully that won't confuse anybody.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.520
Hopefully you all grew up on this version.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.000
And... Mike, what's the name of the game that this is a port of?

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.080
What is the Japanese name of this game?

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.080
Oh, I thought it was Renegade.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.780
What is it? I can't pronounce it.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.200
Oh, yeah. Oh, I know which one you're talking about.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.740
Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah. It's like, Niketsu Koa Kuni Ko.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.380
Kuni Okon. Niketsu Koa Kuni Okon.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.620
Yeah. And Kuni Okon is, I guess, like the person you play as in the game.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.880
I don't know what Niketsu Koa means, but...

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.320
I think it actually...

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.340
That's pretty cool. The translation is very, like, hardcore.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.620
It's like, I will beat your ass on the subway.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:05.740
Something like that.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:13.580
That's cool. We should talk about the objective of this game, which is basically a fist fight through four stages

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:19.160
where you have to take down punks and self-described in the manual thugs along the way.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.160
I didn't want to say the word thugs.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:26.740
They made me. I think, I'm pretty certain, this is our first NES beat-em-up.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.460
Yeah, I think so.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:36.060
And what I find interesting about the objective is that it's also the first time that your objective is just to defend yourself.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.300
Yeah, you could see it that way.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.800
I think most games are that.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.240
But most games, like, the objective is to save someone or to go find something.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.060
You've got to save your girlfriend or you've got to go find this thing.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.040
This is just like, these people just attacked you.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.120
You were just minding your business.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.560
So you're getting jumped in this game over and over.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.080
You're getting jumped in this game at 2 a.m.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.040
on the subway.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.600
And that's where it all starts.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.860
And then you just proceed to continue to walk through various places where you continue to get beat up.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:04.920
Right. Long story short.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.540
That's where it gets a little confusing. You got jumped.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.820
How are you in this building now?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:08.880
How are you? Right.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.940
What made this guy so badass that he became a target?

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.520
The whole city. Are all the people from all the different stages, like, in cahoots with each other?

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.180
Maybe. We'll get to the bottom of that.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.460
But first, I just wanted to talk about the beat-em-up genre in general because this is our first one.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.800
Sean, can you describe what a beat-em-up is?

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.100
Turtles in Time? Yeah, Turtles in Time.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.320
There you go. Perfect. In fact, most Turtles games, that's a fantastic beat-em-up.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.580
Joe, you know any beat-em-ups?

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.920
I'll go the route of, like, actually giving a description of what it is, which is-

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.160
I thought that was a good enough description.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.580
No, I think that is good, but I don't have a good example.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.940
So, I'm going to say it's games where you beat them up.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.520
Okay, wow. That's fantastic.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:53.380
You have to beat up people. It's basically, instead of a side-scroller in which you have two axes of walking, in this

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.660
one you have, no, I'm sorry, one axis of walking in a side-scroller.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:04.420
You have two. You can walk to the back of the screen, the front of the screen, and left and right.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:13.580
And you have, like, a much more tactile, I guess I don't really know the correct way

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.040
to describe the fighting.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.140
I'll give it a shot here.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:23.300
Alright, so, a beat-em-up is a free-roaming game where the enemies are also free to roam

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.380
around the stage. You have to kind of seek them out while they simultaneously seek you out.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:33.440
And the idea behind, you know, most beat-em-ups is that you have to clear off all the enemies

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.160
on the stage for the level to progress.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.780
Yeah. So, sometimes that is locking you to a screen before the screen will continue to scroll.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.740
In this particular instance, it's a little more involved in that.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:51.620
It's actually, like, a door will open or you'll have to get on a subway or get on bikes.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:52.940
Yeah, for like a minute.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.120
For a hot minute.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:00.880
But I think that that's nice to have a different variety because we've seen what could have

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.360
been beat-em-ups before with games like Kung Fu and stuff like that had they have just,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.860
you know, taken on those concepts instead.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:14.740
But because now you're finally free to roam around the stage, there's kind of like some

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.380
choice involved on your own part of how you're going to tackle the enemies.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.260
You don't have to clear the enemy directly in front of you to get to the next one.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:23.980
It plays a little bit more expressive.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.180
Yes. And you can be as aggressive or as defensive as you want.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.680
And so, it sounds like Joe went defensive.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.420
Yeah. I didn't actually. He just curled up into a little wall.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.600
I'm just talking about the way the manual describes it.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:37.800
It says that you're just defending yourself.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.600
So, do you want to give your first impression?

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.920
Yeah. Actually, the first thing that I thought of was that this is a very, the backgrounds in this

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.040
are very nice looking.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.360
They're very clean and very, there's, there's, there's a lot of

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.560
very clear signs in the background and the subway looks nice.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.020
It is a, it is a nice looking game off the bat.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.920
Okay. Sean, first impressions?

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:07.100
Um, my first impression was, was sort of, uh, in that same vein where I did appreciate

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.410
that, uh, the backgrounds had more context.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:19.450
They seemed to be based on actual places instead of, uh, a labyrinth that had like windows in

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:20.370
places. You know what I mean?

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.570
Um, I did like that the, the stages were real places.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:28.250
Um, that was really all I saw at first though.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:33.030
So, and my first impression, which seems to be a little different from yours is that for

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:39.610
both of your guys, uh, this is what fighting in your dreams is like, like when you have

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:43.990
a dream and all of a sudden you start throwing punches, but they don't do anything and everybody

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:45.670
can reach them.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.930
Uh, that's, that's my impression from here.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:53.310
Uh, because I, I had a difficult time playing this one.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.910
Uh, I did get a little better as time went on.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.330
So I'm not saying the game is awful.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:01.190
I'm saying that, uh, definitely my, you know, you got to learn this one.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:04.610
Uh, it's not, uh, it's not all there laid out for you.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:09.470
It's not easy by any means, uh, throughout the entirety of the game, to be honest, had there

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:15.890
have just been maybe a little more, um, simplified control scheme, which we'll get to, uh, this

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:19.810
could have been a winner. First, we have to talk about one thing in the manual that I noticed that I thought was

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:21.990
very odd in the manual.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:26.490
Uh, there's a description of like each stage and stuff like that, and then how to control

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:30.910
your characters and then different things that you can do, uh, with your character.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:35.250
And then there's just like this page that's just like cheats to get to stage two and three.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:42.330
Mm-hmm . . . If you're tired of dying on stage one all the time, here's a, you can press this combination

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:48.130
of buttons on controller one and two, and you'll get to immediately go to either stage

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:49.430
two or stage three.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:54.550
They will not let you go to the final stage, stage four, uh, by means of cheats.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:58.490
It was interesting to me though, and I think this has actually happened before and I just

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:03.690
didn't know about it, but that you have to hold one button down on controller one while

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.590
putting the inputs in controller two.

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.030
Yeah. I didn't know that was even a function.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:14.470
I think the function of that, um, is to make it, make the lazy people not cheat because

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:19.430
I was lazy enough, because I did end up wanting to just skip because I kept dying.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.290
Right. Um, uh, but I, that was just too much for me.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.170
So I ended up playing it over again.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.310
Yeah, there's a, there's another implementation implementation that we didn't talk about in

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:35.090
the Karnov episode where if you are player two on Karnov, you just hold down A and B and

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:38.790
like after like, I think a few seconds, Karnov will just automatically die.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:40.990
What? Yeah.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:42.290
It's like, it's just to.

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:43.330
It's like a soft lock thing.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:44.270
Like a soft lock. Yeah.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.330
Reset yourself and back to the beginning of that level.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:49.410
And crazy stuff.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:50.430
But we didn't talk about it.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:54.050
So we're going to mention it now because that's the only instance we're ever going

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.310
to have to bring that up again.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.410
We'll talk about Karnov in every episode going forward.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:58.590
Hope. Well, he's our mascot.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:01.530
All right. Now we're talking about Renegade.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:02.770
Let's talk about gameplay.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:05.510
I am going to bring up my biggest issue.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:11.070
First and foremost, guys, why did they reverse punch and kick depending on direction?

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:19.190
See, I think it's a good idea in like on paper, like, context controls.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:26.370
I think the controls are big now where everything sort of depends on what you're doing as a

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:31.950
player character. But for this, it doesn't, it just, it feels like your wires are getting crossed every time

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:32.950
you turn around. Yeah.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:36.250
I think because it's punching and kicking, I think what they were trying to do was there

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.330
was a shmup that we played like this where it was directional.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:45.110
Section Z was like, you can use this button to turn around and then that, this button for

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:45.990
shooting. No, no, no.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:52.210
It was another one where I think it was the one when you're in the jet pack where A shoots

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:53.770
to the right and B shoots to the left.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:54.830
Yeah. That's section Z.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:55.870
It is section Z. Okay.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:03.630
So then the, in this, I think it was trying to do that, but then making one direction,

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.870
the direction you're facing, the punch and the other direction, a kick just makes it

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.170
so much more confusing.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:10.950
This is just an archaic design choice though.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:15.490
For me personally, like I can't understand this because we've had plenty of games now

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:21.210
that understand that like direction and actions don't, shouldn't change.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:27.670
Like there's no, for me, there's no rationale why, why punch and kick should change depending

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.890
on the direction you're standing in.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.610
Right. Well, it's the directionality of the attack though.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.730
Like the kick only happens behind you.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:36.850
Right. It's yeah.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:38.830
So, so imagine if it was just punch.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:40.150
A is the punch to the right.

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:41.230
B is the punch to the left.

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:45.290
I feel like that's just, that's makes more sense in your head right away when you tell

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:49.870
it, when they tell you that the manual says something like, you know, it says when facing

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.130
the right, A is the punch, B is the kick.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:54.610
When facing the left, B is the punch, A is the kick.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:58.230
Like that just, just say punch to the right by hitting A, punch to the left by hitting

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:00.230
B. But that's not what it is though, right?

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.690
Yeah. Attack to the left by hitting A, attack to the right by hitting B.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:08.250
It's just that why would, look, I just can't, I really can't forgive the game for this.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.350
I'm not going to get on it. I don't disagree either.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:16.150
I just don't, the kick button can, if kick button stayed to be B at all times, right?

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:20.530
Let's just say that. Whether you were facing either direction, I get that it kicks from behind.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.550
Why would that matter though?

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.770
Like, why would you have to change that?

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:25.970
I don't know. There's no reason, right?

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:31.450
The only kinds of games when I can see like this, this make any sense is like twin stick

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:33.110
shooters or stuff like Smash TV.

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:36.070
Right. But that is a completely different kind of game.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:37.930
And this isn't it.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.710
Does the kick do anything?

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:41.250
Does it have a greater distance?

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.350
I feel it's a bit stronger and it has a wider.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:49.150
I found, I found the kick to be like a better option if you can, if you can remember which,

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:50.950
which button it is at that particular time.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:54.890
Uh, I also thought that like the jump kicks were the best thing in the game.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.930
Sometimes. That's when you hit A and B together.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:03.970
So I just kind of defaulted to that because I couldn't bear to remember like which buttons

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:05.430
I needed to press. I'm being serious.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:08.670
Like I really got confused by this to the point where I was almost like I didn't want

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.070
to play the game.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:14.150
I couldn't wire my brain to remember like, oh, facing this direction, better use this button.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.890
My solution to that was to never think I'll have to kick this guy off to punch this guy.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.030
It was just if they're behind me, if they're behind me, I hit A or whichever.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:22.870
If they're behind me, I hit A. If they're in front of me, I hit B.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:24.330
And just ignore whether it's kicking or punching.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.970
No matter which way I'm facing, just press the button in the direction of the enemy.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:34.210
Yeah. Which is flawed because then you don't, you don't think about kicking and punching.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:37.830
I ended up being able to get a little okay at this game.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:42.470
But that was, again, it was completely unintuitive because of that though.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:46.250
And in terms of priority, right?

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:51.130
When it's you and the enemy hitting at the same time, who's got the priority here?

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:58.050
I feel like the enemies really get like, especially the ones with weapons have like a major priority

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:05.850
when it comes to registering attacks, which is upsetting because you're taking on like four to five enemies at a time.

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:07.790
And they do gang up on you.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:10.090
So you could be in a grapple with one of them.

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:14.690
And then the guy with the bat just comes over and whacks you with it and you fall to the ground.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.670
Yeah. It's simulating an actual street fight.

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.870
Yes. And to that, I give it some credit.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:23.870
Sure. Like, no, it did feel like I was definitely in a losing street fight.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:31.050
Like, there's definitely like a thousand simulations where the main character dies because he can't fight five guys at once.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:37.750
But a lot of times when I'm in an actual street fight, the guy in front of me, I'll be accidentally kicking behind me and not realizing I'm trying to punch the guy in front of me and I forget.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.390
It's like what fighting in your dreams is like.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:45.410
Yeah, that's true. I really found that bat guy in stage one to be absolutely ridiculous though.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:50.930
My thing for him was jump kicks working and I would take him out first.

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:57.510
Mm-hmm. I felt like it was a losing strategy to just leave him with the bat and just try to deal with other people.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:13:59.710
Yeah, because you're right. His attack is so fast.

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:01.450
You can't get near him if he's about to attack.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:03.670
Mm-hmm. All of those weaponed boats.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:11.090
I think the weapon holding characters are actually weaker though in terms of how many times they have to get knocked down.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.030
Yeah, which is great.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:17.370
You know, it's a good design decision from the game to make something happen.

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.790
And I'm not saying that it took a ton of jump kicks.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:29.350
I think that, you know, if anything, like the jump kicks on weapons makes even more sense if you think about it because you're not going to punch some guy with a bat.

